<!--
  Prototype Rollout Plan - Group meeting schedule.
  Drop into docs/source/ and xi:include from main.ptx.
  Cross-referenced via xml:id="ch-rollout-plan".
-->

<chapter xml:id="ch-using-the-tool" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Pipeline Rollout &amp; User Guide</title>

  <introduction>
    <warning>
      <p>
        WORK IN PROGRESS
      </p>
    </warning>

    <p>
      <term>Purpose.</term> This document describes the current state of
      the automation tool prototype and serves as a space for learning about
      and discussing the direction of its development. It is organized like
      a tutorial. So feel free to refer back to it whenever you need to
      recall the steps or understand how a particular feature works.
      For the overall design
      strategy, see the <xref ref="ch-automation-tool-roadmap" text="title"/>.
    </p>

    <p>
      <term>Where this fits.</term> The tool (PROTEUS) is organized as a
      service-agnostic core&#x2014;shared ingest and storage&#x2014;with one
      service per question type built on top of it. This rollout covers the
      <term>short-answer</term> service; a separate drag-and-drop service is in
      progress over the same core. The ingest step described below belongs to
      the shared core and feeds every service, while the screening, extraction,
      matching, and disposition steps are short-answer-specific.
    </p>

    <p>
      <term>Working assumptions.</term> The prototype is in an early-stage and 
      open to change: column choices, filter behavior, the preview-then-commit 
      workflow, and even the basic premise of what the ingest stage should do 
      are all up for discussion. Nothing about the current implementation should 
      be treated as final, and group feedback is the primary input determining 
      what is kept, changed, or removed.
    </p>

    <p>
      The prototype is made up of three main components: 
      <dl>
        <li>
          <title>Command-line Tool</title>
          <p>
            Written in Python, the command-line tool drives the 
            automation tool's core functionality.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Dashboard</title>
          <p>
            Built with the Streamlit module, the dashboard provides 
            a web-based interface for researchers to interact with, 
            modify, and manage the data.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Database</title>
          <p>
            The data is stored in a small <xref ref="an-sqlite" text="custom">SQLite</xref> 
            database file.
          </p>
        </li>
      </dl> 
    </p>

    <p>
      The prototype is temporarily hosted on a public Streamlit cloud service and 
      is accessible through a browser. This allows group members to access the tool 
      from anywhere, but the database will be deleted once the frontend is closed. 
      If the tool is adopted, a different hosting option will need to be arranged.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- Section: Ingesting Runestone Data                            -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="sec-ingest-rs-data">
    <title>Ingesting Runestone Data</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        This section demonstrates the ingest stage of the tool. This stage is composed of three steps:
        <ul>
          <li>importing a raw data file into the frontend,</li>
          <li>previewing the imported data, and</li>
          <li>committing the data to the database.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- Subsection: Opening the Tool &amp; Homepage Overview         -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-opening-frontend">
      <title>Opening the Frontend</title>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <url href="https://cs-pipeline-prototype.streamlit.app/">Click here</url> 
            to open the tool in your browser.
          </li>
          <li>Wait for the page to load and wake it up if it is sleeping.</li>
          <li>
            <p>
              The first page is the Homepage Screen, which shows: 
              <ul>
                <li>A title and tool description.</li>
                <li>A sidebar for navigation and settings. The sidebar can be hidden by pressing <kbd>&lt;&lt;</kbd> and expanded by pressing <kbd>&gt;&gt;</kbd></li>
                <li>Some database information.</li>
                <li>Four counts: imports, responses, runs, and screened.</li>
              </ul>
              The counts describe what is currently in the database. As files are ingested, 
              the first two counts grow; the remaining two will become meaningful in later 
              stages and can be ignored for now.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- Subsection: Importing a File                                 -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-import-file">
      <title>Importing a Runstone File</title>

      <p>
        The Ingest page accepts a single export file at a time. Two formats
        are supported: 
        <ul>
          <li>
            <xref ref="an-runestone" text="custom">Runestone</xref>
            generated <xref ref="an-datashop" text="custom">DataShop</xref>
            (<c>.tab</c>) files, and
          </li>
          <li>CSV files.</li>
        </ul>
        Once you have a file ready to import, proceed with the following steps.
      </p>

      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>Navigate to the Ingest page from the sidebar.</li>
          <li>Select a file to ingest using the file picker.</li>
          <li>Wait for the file to upload. A confirmation message names the file and its size.</li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <p>
        The file picker only accepts the two supported formats; other
        file types are rejected immediately so that no unsupported input
        ever reaches the parser. <em>Uploading the file does not change the
        database.</em>
      </p>

    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- Subsection: Previewing an Import                             -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-preview-import">
      <title>Previewing an Import</title>

      <p>
        Once a file is uploaded it can be previewed through the following steps.
        <ol marker="1.">
          <li>
            <p>
              Press the <kbd>Parse &amp; Preview</kbd> button. The four metrics appear: 
              rows parsed, new responses, duplicates, and unique students.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Inspect the per-question summary that lists each <c>question_id</c> in the 
              file and how many responses are associated with it.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Scroll the response table to spot-check the parsed text against the raw file 
              if desired.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Toggle the <em>Hide duplicates</em> checkbox to filter the preview table down 
              to responses that would actually be written. A file with a large number of 
              duplicates against the existing database usually means the file overlaps an 
              earlier export, and is worth a closer look before committing.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- Subsection: Reading the Preview (warnings &amp; columns)     -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-preview-warnings-columns">
      <title>Parser Warnings and Preview Columns</title>

      <p>
        While previewing, the parser may report that it skipped one or more
        rows it could not read. These warnings are grouped by reason and shown
        above the response table as short summary lines, with a
        <em>Show all skipped rows</em> expander beneath that lists every
        skipped row and its reason.
      </p>

      <p>
        A rule of thumb for reading them:
        <ul>
          <li>Each warning corresponds to exactly one row the parser skipped.</li>
          <li>
            A handful of skipped rows in a large export is normal and a few
            stray or malformed events rarely matter.
          </li>
          <li>
            If <em>every</em> row is skipped (the parsed-rows count is zero), then
            the file has a format problem worth fixing before committing. A
            common cause is a non-standard date column the timestamp parser
            does not yet recognise, which makes every row fail the same way.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Two columns in the preview deserve a note, since the group asked what
        they are for:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <c>course_id</c> is an optional field from the export that can identify
            the course, instructor, or term a response came from. When the export
            omits a course identifier a placeholder is shown. It is not analytically
            central; it is kept so a response can be traced back to its source.
          </li>
          <li>
            <c>response_id</c> is the deterministic hash of the student,
            question, and timestamp. It is the stable key that makes
            re-imports idempotent and powers the duplicate detection described
            above: the same response always produces the same
            <c>response_id</c>, so re-importing it is recognised and skipped
            rather than duplicated.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- Subsection: Committing an Import                             -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-commit-import">
      <title>Committing an Import</title>

      <p>
        Commit is the step that writes parsed responses to the database. Once committed, 
        the imported file is recorded in the <c>raw_imports</c> table and its responses 
        appear in the <c>responses</c> table; both are visible on the Imports page from
        that point forward.
        <ol marker="1.">
          <li>
            An optional note can be added to any import. To test this, add something to 
            the "Notes" field for the current import.
          </li>
          <li>
            Press the <em>Commit</em> button. The button is only enabled after a preview 
            has been generated.
          </li>
          <li>
            Wait for the result panel to appear. It reports the number of new responses 
            written, the number of duplicates skipped, and a unique identifier for the 
            import.
          </li>
          <li>
            Return to the home page and confirm that the imports and responses counts 
            have increased.The counts on the home page provide a quick sanity check: if 
            a file with 46 new responses was just committed, the responses count should 
            have increased by exactly that amount.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>

    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- Subsection: Re-importing the same file                       -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-reimport">
      <title>Re-importing the Same File</title>

      <p>
        The tool is designed to make re-ingestion safe both at the file level and the 
        row level. That is, the tool blocks:
        <ul>
          <li>The same file from being uploaded</li>
          <li>
            Individual rows from being uploaded if they already exist in the database, 
            even if they arrive in a different file
          </li>
        </ul>
        This allows group members to upload, re-upload, and try variations without 
        worrying about accidental damage.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can see this in action through the following steps.
        <ol marker="1.">
          <li><p>Return to the Ingest page.</p></li>
          <li><p>Select the same file that was just committed.</p></li>
          <li><p>Press <em>Preview</em>. A warning appears noting that the file has already been ingested.</p></li>
          <li><p>If preview is still attempted, the duplicates count will equal the total parsed rows and the new-responses count will be zero.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </subsection>

  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- Section: Managing Imports                                    -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="sec-managing-imports">
    <title>Managing Imports</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        This section demonstrates the import management stage of the tool. This stage 
        is equipped with the following functionality:
        <ul>
          <li>Viewing a list of all imported files.</li>
          <li>Deleting individual imports.</li>
          <li>Filtering imports based on various criteria.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- Subsection: Browsing past imports                            -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-meeting-1-browsing-imports">
      <title>Browsing Past Imports</title>

      <p>
        The Imports page provides a view of every file that has ever been ingested. The master 
        list is the entry point for finding any particular file or any particular response. Here 
        is a sample workflow:
        <ol marker="1.">
          <li>Navigate to the Imports page from the sidebar.</li>
          <li>
            Scan the master list. Each row corresponds to one ingest event and shows the file name, 
            the number of responses, and a timestamp.
          </li>
          <li>
            Click the row of the file just committed. A detail panel opens to its right (or below, 
            depending on window width).
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- Subsection: Filtering responses                              -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-meeting-1-filtering">
      <title>Filtering Responses Within an Import</title>

      <p>
        The detail panel of an import contains a list of all responses
        loaded from that file, together with filters for narrowing the view
        to a particular subset.
        <ol marker="1.">
          <li>With an import selected, locate the filter controls above the response list.</li>
          <li>
            Use the <c>question_id</c> dropdown to restrict the view to a single question. The 
            response count updates to reflect the filtered total.
          </li>
          <li>
            Optionally, use the text search box to find responses containing a particular word 
            or phrase.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <paragraphs>
        <title>Comments</title>
        <p>
          The filters affect only what is displayed, not what is stored. 
          Clearing the filter restores the full view.
        </p>
      </paragraphs>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- Subsection: Drilling into a response                         -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-meeting-1-drilling">
      <title>Drilling Into a Single Response</title>

      <p>
        Each row in the response list expands to show the full response
        text. Where a question previewer has been configured for the
        question that the response answers, the rendered question is
        displayed alongside the response so that the two can be read
        together.
        <ol marker="1.">
          <li>Click the expander on any response row in the filtered list.</li>
          <li>Read the full response text in the expanded panel.</li>
          <li>
            A question previewer is currently being developed. If it is in the tool, you 
            can click on it to reveal the question that is selected.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- Subsection: Exporting to CSV                                 -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-meeting-1-export">
      <title>Exporting a View to CSV</title>

      <p>
        Any view of the responses can be exported as a CSV file. The export
        respects the current filter state, so an export taken after
        filtering by <c>question_id</c> contains only the responses for
        that question.
        <ol marker="1.">
          <li>Apply any desired filters to the response list.</li>
          <li>Press the <em>Download CSV</em> button above the list.</li>
          <li>
            Open the downloaded file in a spreadsheet program to confirm the export 
            contains the expected rows.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <paragraphs>
        <title>Comments</title>
        <p>
          Export is a one-way operation: it produces a file for the user to keep, edit, 
          or share, but the tool will not re-ingest exported files. Anything edited in 
          the spreadsheet stays in the spreadsheet. The export is provided so that 
          researchers who prefer to work with a familiar spreadsheet format are free to 
          do so.
        </p>
      </paragraphs>
    </subsection>

  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- SECTION: Screening Student Responses                         -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="sec-screening-student-responses">
    <title>Screening Student Responses</title>

    <remark>
      <title>Section status</title>
      <p>
        Placeholder. This section will be drafted after Meeting 1 has been
        held and its outcomes recorded. The intended focus is the
        eligibility-screening stage, which decides which responses are
        candidates for control-structure coding and which are filtered out
        as blank, off-topic, or otherwise non-codable. Anchor decisions for
        this meeting will be drafted from the categories of responses the
        group most wants to control at the screening boundary.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </section>

